  rightFooterText: {fileID: 260129041}
  currentPage: 1
  maxPages: 12
  topics:
  - topicTag: TOPIC 1
    topicTitle: "Early Life & Education of Jos\xE9 Rizal"
    leftSubtitle: Calamba & Early Studies
    leftBody: "Jos\xE9 Rizal was born on <b>June 19, 1861</b>, in Calamba, Laguna.
      His mother, <b>Teodora Alonso Realonda</b>, was one of his first teachers.
      She taught him how to read and pray at a young age.\n\nRizal later continued
      his education in Bi\xF1an, where <b>Maestro Justiniano Aquino Cruz</b> became
      his first formal school teacher."
    rightSubtitle: Medical Training & European Travels
    rightBody: 'Rizal later studied medicine because he wanted to help his mother
      with her failing eyesight. He pursued <b>ophthalmology</b>, the branch of medicine
      concerned with the eyes.


      He continued his studies in Europe, including
      at the <b>Universidad Central de Madrid</b> and later trained in ophthalmology
      under <b>Dr. Otto Becker</b> in the University of Heidelberg in Germany.


      His
      travels also allowed him to encounter different cultures, literature, and languages.
      Among these was <b>German</b>, which he learned during his time in Europe.
      His experiences abroad expanded his knowledge and influenced his later writings
      and ideas about the Philippines.'
  - topicTag: TOPIC 2
    topicTitle: Literary Masterpieces & Writing
    leftSubtitle: Noli Me Tangere
    leftBody: "Jos\xE9 Rizal used literature to express his observations about Philippine
      society during Spanish colonial rule. His two novels, <i>Noli Me Tangere</i>
      and <i>El Filibusterismo</i>, presented stories that showed the struggles and
      injustices experienced by Filipinos during that period.\n\nRizal\u2019s first
      novel, <i>Noli Me Tangere</i>, was published in Berlin. Its publication was
      made possible through the financial support of <color=#1B6833><b>Maximo Viola</b></color>,
      who helped Rizal when he faced difficulties in printing the novel.\n\nThe story
      is set in the fictional town of San Diego, where characters such as <color=#1B6833><b>Sisa</b></color>
      represent the suffering experienced by ordinary Filipinos."
    rightSubtitle: "El Filibusterismo & Mi \xDAltimo Adi\xF3s"
    rightBody: "His second novel, <i>El Filibusterismo</i>, continued his criticism
      of colonial abuses and social injustice. It was published in <color=#1B6833><b>Ghent,
      Belgium</b></color>, with <color=#1B6833><b>Valentin Ventura</b></color> providing
      financial assistance that helped complete its printing.\n\nRizal also wrote
      <i>Mi \xDAltimo Adi\xF3s</i>, a farewell poem expressing his love for his country.
      Before his execution, he secretly placed the manuscript inside an <color=#1B6833><b>alcohol
      cooking stove</b></color> and entrusted it to his sister Trinidad Rizal."
  - topicTag: TOPIC 3
    topicTitle: Propaganda Movement & Nationalist Essays
    leftSubtitle: Patriotic Press & Assimilation
    leftBody: "In 1882, Rizal\u2019s first patriotic essay, <i>El Amor Patrio</i>
      (Love of Country), was published in <color=#1B6833><b>Diariong Tagalog</b></color>,
      a bilingual newspaper in Manila.\n\nLater, he became involved with <i>La Solidaridad</i>,
      a reformist newspaper that promoted <color=#1B6833><b>assimilation with Spain</b></color>.
      Filipino reformists wanted political and social reforms, including greater
      representation in the Spanish government.\n\nIn 1872, the tragic event of the
      <color=#1B6833><b>execution of GOMBURZA</b></color> awakened young Rizal\u2019s
      sense of love for his country and strengthened his desire to understand the
      injustices faced by Filipinos under Spanish colonial rule."
    rightSubtitle: Malolos Letter & Future Vision
    rightBody: "The Malolos Letter reflected ideas about education and the role of
      women in society. Written by Rizal in response to the young women of Malolos,
      the letter encouraged Filipino mothers to instill <color=#1B6833><b>love for
      the country</b></color> in their children.\n\nRizal also wrote <i>The Philippines
      a Century Hence</i>, a series of essays that examined the country\u2019s past
      and possible future. The work considered how foreign powers, including the
      <color=#1B6833><b>United States</b></color>, might affect the Philippines."
  - topicTag: TOPIC 4
    topicTitle: La Liga Filipina & Civic Reforms
    leftSubtitle: Founding & Objectives
    leftBody: "On July 3, 1892, Jos\xE9 Rizal founded <b>La Liga Filipina</b> in
      Tondo, Manila.\n\nIt was a civic organization that aimed to unite Filipinos
      and encourage cooperation among its members.\n\nOne of the primary objectives
      stated in its constitution was to provide <color=#1B6833><b>mutual protection
      among its members</b></color>."
    rightSubtitle: Arrest, Exile & Successors
    rightBody: "However, Rizal\u2019s involvement with La Liga was short-lived. Shortly
      after its founding, Spanish Governor General <color=#1B6833><b>Eulogio Despujol</b></color>
      ordered his arrest and deportation. Rizal was subsequently sent to Dapitan.\n\nAfter
      Rizal\u2019s arrest, <color=#1B6833><b>Andres Bonifacio</b></color>, who had
      been a member of La Liga, later became one of the founders of the Katipunan.\n\nWhen
      La Liga was reorganized, <color=#1B6833><b>Apolinario Mabini</b></color> served
      as secretary of its Supreme Council. After the organization eventually dissolved,
      the <color=#1B6833><b>Cuerpo de Compromisarios</b></color> continued efforts
      to support <i>La Solidaridad</i>."
  - topicTag: TOPIC 5
    topicTitle: Dapitan Exile & Community Leadership
    leftSubtitle: Community Medicine & Education
    leftBody: "Jos\xE9 Rizal spent part of his exile in Dapitan, where he became
      more than a physician. He used his knowledge and skills to serve the local
      community.\n\nAs a doctor, he successfully operated on his mother, <color=#1B6833><b>Teodora
      Alonso Realonda</b></color>, for cataracts.\n\nRizal also established a school
      in Talisay, where he personally taught around <color=#1B6833><b>sixteen young
      male</b></color> students without charging tuition."
    rightSubtitle: Innovations, Science & Medical Mission
    rightBody: 'He had also worked on projects that improved the community. He introduced
      <color=#1B6833><b>coconut oil streetlamps</b></color> to illuminate the streets
      of Dapitan.


      His interests extended to science and agriculture. A horned
      beetle discovered by Rizal in Dapitan was scientifically named <color=#1B6833><b>Apogonia
      rizali</b></color> in his honor.


      In 1896, Dr. Pio Valenzuela, a Katipunan
      emissary, secretly visited Rizal in Dapitan. Rizal later requested permission
      to serve as a military surgeon in a <color=#1B6833><b>Spanish army hospital
      in Cuba</b></color>. He hoped to use his medical skills to provide humanitarian
      medical assistance to wounded soldiers and patients.'
  - topicTag: TOPIC 6
    topicTitle: Spanish Colonial Administration & Institutions
    leftSubtitle: Central Government & Judicial Court
    leftBody: 'During the Spanish colonial period, the Philippines was governed under
      a centralized colonial system.


      The <color=#1B6833><b>Governor-General</b></color>
      was the highest colonial official and represented the Spanish Monarch in the
      Philippines.


      The <color=#1B6833><b>Real Audiencia</b></color> served
      as the highest judicial court and also advised the Governor-General.'
    rightSubtitle: Frailocracia & Economic Extraction
    rightBody: 'Spanish religious orders also had considerable influence over colonial
      affairs. The term <color=#1B6833><b>frailocracia</b></color> was used to describe
      the political and social dominance of Spanish friars during the colonial period.


      The
      colonial government also used economic systems to collect resources and support
      its administration. Under the <color=#1B6833><b>bandala system</b></color>,
      farmers were required to sell agricultural products to the government at fixed
      prices.


      The government also received financial support through the
      <color=#1B6833><b>Real Situado</b></color>, an annual financial grant from
      New Spain, or present-day Mexico.'
  - topicTag: TOPIC 7
    topicTitle: The Propaganda Movement & La Solidaridad
    leftSubtitle: Reformist Press & La Solidaridad
    leftBody: "The Propaganda Movement was a reform movement led by Filipino intellectuals
      and reformists, many of whom worked from Spain. They used writing to call for
      reforms in the Philippines.\n\nOne of their major publications was <i>La Solidaridad</i>,
      first published in Barcelona in 1889 with <color=#1B6833><b>Graciano L\xF3pez
      Jaena</b></color> as its founding editor-in-chief."
    rightSubtitle: Satire, Arts & Madrid Headquarters
    rightBody: "The movement also used literature and satire to criticize abuses
      during the Spanish colonial period. L\xF3pez Jaena wrote <color=#1B6833><b>Fray
      Botod</b></color>, which criticizes the abuse of Spanish friars. In 1884, <color=#1B6833><b>Juan
      Luna</b></color> won a first-class gold medal at the Madrid Fine Arts Exposition
      for his painting <i>Spolarium</i>.\n\nIn late 1889, Marcelo H. del Pilar moved
      the headquarters of the reformists to <color=#1B6833><b>Madrid</b></color>
      to be closer to the Spanish Cortes, supported by the <color=#1B6833><b>Junta
      de Propaganda</b></color>. However, publication ceased in 1895 due to financial
      difficulties and the declining health of its leaders."
  - topicTag: TOPIC 8
    topicTitle: The Katipunan & The Philippine Revolution
    leftSubtitle: Cry of Pugad Lawin & Initiation
    leftBody: "In August 1896, members of the Katipunan rejected Spanish authority
      during the Cry of Pugad Lawin by <color=#1B6833><b>tearing their c\xE9dulas</b></color>,
      symbolizing their rejection of colonial rule.\n\nNew members also underwent
      initiation rituals, including a <color=#1B6833><b>blood compact or blood-signing
      ceremony</b></color> to show their commitment to the organization."
    rightSubtitle: Revolutionary Government & Battles
    rightBody: 'The revolutionary movement later experienced political divisions.
      At the Tejeros Convention in March 1897, <color=#1B6833><b>Emilio Aguinaldo</b></color>
      was elected president of the revolutionary government.


      On August 30,
      1896, Katipunan forces fought the Spanish army in the <color=#1B6833><b>Battle
      of San Juan del Monte</b></color>.


      In 1899, <color=#1B6833><b>Gregorio
      del Pillar</b></color> led Filipino forces in the Battle of Tirad Pass, where
      they held off American troops.'
  - topicTag: TOPIC 9
    topicTitle: Precolonial Culture, Artifacts & Traditions
    leftSubtitle: Textiles & Pintados
    leftBody: "Long before Spanish colonization, communities throughout the Philippine
      Islands already had their own identity and cultures.\n\nThe <color=#1B6833><b>T\u2019boli
      people</b></color> of South Cotabato are known for weaving <i>T\u2019nalak</i>,
      a sacred textile made from abaca fibers.\n\nIn Visayan communities, the Spanish
      chroniclers referred to them as \u201CPintados\u201D due to their <color=#1B6833><b>painted
      bodies</b></color>."
    rightSubtitle: Writing, Carvings & Music
    rightBody: 'Filipinos also developed writing systems before Spanish colonization.
      <color=#1B6833><b>Baybayin</b></color> was an indigenous syllabic script used
      commonly by the Tagalog-speaking groups.


      Other important traditions
      include the Ifugao <color=#1B6833><b>bulul</b></color>, a carved wooden figure
      associated with rice and protection of the harvest, and traditional <color=#1B6833><b>kulintang</b></color>
      music, which uses a set of tuned gongs.'
  - topicTag: TOPIC 10
    topicTitle: Philippine Mythology & Folklore
    leftSubtitle: Deities & Celestial Lore
    leftBody: 'Philippine mythology and folklore contain many stories about supernatural
      beings, deities, heroes, and creatures. These stories vary between different
      cultural communities and regions of the Philippines.


      In Tagalog mythology,
      Bathala was regarded as a supreme creator deity. His daughter <color=#1B6833><b>Tala</b></color>
      was associated with the stars, while Mayari was associated with the moon. Stories
      about these deities helped explain natural phenomena and the world around people.'
    rightSubtitle: Legendary Creatures of Folklore
    rightBody: 'Different regions also have their own legendary creatures:


      The
      <color=#1B6833><b>Tikbalang</b></color> is commonly described as a tall humanoid
      creature with the head and legs of a horse.


      The <color=#1B6833><b>Kapre</b></color>
      is traditionally described as a large tree-dwelling being often portrayed smoking
      a huge cigar.


      Other traditions include the <color=#1B6833><b>Bakunawa</b></color>,
      a gigantic serpent-like creature in Visayan mythology associated with eclipses,
      and the <color=#1B6833><b>Sarimanok</b></color>, a colorful legendary bird
      associated with the Maranao people.'
  - topicTag: TOPIC 11
    topicTitle: Philippine Historical Landmarks & Heritage
    leftSubtitle: Indigenous Terraces & Burial Jars
    leftBody: "The Philippines has many historical places, objects, structures, and
      traditions that help preserve the country\u2019s past. These forms of heritage
      show the creativity, beliefs, and experiences of Filipino communities across
      different periods.\n\nThe <color=#1B6833><b>Banaue Rice Terraces</b></color>
      represent the agricultural knowledge and efforts of Indigenous Cordillerian
      communities.\n\n<color=#1B6833><b>The Manunggul Jar</b></color>, discovered
      in Palawan, is a prehistoric burial jar known for the boat and two human figures
      depicted on its lid, representing a journey toward the afterlife."
    rightSubtitle: Mindanao Pots & Colonial Sites
    rightBody: "In Mindanao, the <color=#1B6833><b>Maitum Anthropomorphic Pots</b></color>
      are ancient burial jars with lids shaped with human faces.\n\nSpanish colonial
      heritage can be seen in places such as <color=#1B6833><b>Paoay Church</b></color>
      in Ilocos Norte, known for its resistance against earthquakes.\n\nThe <color=#1B6833><b>Bamboo
      Organ</b></color> in St. Joseph Church in Las Pi\xF1as is a Philippine heritage
      site created by Father Diego Cera and uses bamboo pipes as part of its unique
      construction."
  - topicTag: TOPIC 12
    topicTitle: Revolutionary Heroes & Key Historical Figures
    leftSubtitle: Founders, Strategists & Generals
    leftBody: "The Philippine struggle for freedom involved many individuals who
      played different roles in the revolutionary movement and conflicts that followed.\n\n<color=#1B6833><b>Andres
      Bonifacio</b></color> was the founder and chief organizer of the Katipunan.
      He was known as the \u201CSupremo\u201D of the organization.\n\n<color=#1B6833><b>General
      Antonio Luna</b></color> served as a military strategist and commander of the
      Philippine Revolutionary Army.\n\n<color=#1B6833><b>Miguel Malvar</b></color>
      was known for his perseverance and became one of the last major Filipino generals
      to formally surrender in Luzon."
    rightSubtitle: Heroines of the Revolution
    rightBody: "Women also played important roles.\n\n<color=#1B6833><b>Melchora
      Aquino</b></color>, known as Tandang Sora, provided food, shelter, and medical
      assistance to revolutionary fighters.\n\nIn the Visayas, <color=#1B6833><b>Teresa
      Magbanua</b></color>, a teacher from Iloilo, became a military leader and was
      later remembered as the \u201CVisayan Joan of Arc\u201D.\n\nThese individuals
      represent different forms of leadership from organizing secret societies and
      writing political ideas to providing medical support and leading soldiers in
      battle."
